    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Software Development & IANA Timezone Handling for Chicago</h2>
      <p>
        When developing global software systems, backend databases, or cloud infrastructure that interacts with users in Illinois or the US Midwest, never hardcode static numeric offsets like <code>-06:00</code> or <code>-05:00</code>. Hardcoded offsets fail to account for historic policy shifts or annual Daylight Saving Time transitions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Always rely on the official IANA time zone string: <code>America/Chicago</code>. This handles daylight transitions automatically across programming languages including Node.js, Python, Java, and C#.
      </p>

      <div class="code-block">
        <pre><code>{`// Example JavaScript ISO 8601 formatting for Chicago
const date = new Date();
const chicagoFormatted = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', {
  timeZone: 'America/Chicago',
  dateStyle: 'full',
  timeStyle: 'long'
}).format(date);

console.log(chicagoFormatted);
// Output: Thursday, July 30, 2026 at 10:45:00 AM CDT`}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ Section -->
    <section class="content-section faq-section">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Chicago Time</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
